; t38_shell.n — v16.2 shell forms and large-output safety: the argv list form
; (no /bin/sh, no quoting), stdout/stderr/status capture for both forms, and
; the GC regression — a >1MB captured output plus a fold inside a collection
; for must not crash or corrupt (signal-11 class bugs).
; Run: nk comp/tests/t38_shell.n

; 1. list form: argv exec, no shell quoting
["echo" "argv-works"] shell out# _# _#
out "argv-works\n" eq check

; 2. list form captures stderr and the exit status separately
["sh" "-c" "echo to-stderr 1>&2; exit 3"] shell out# err# st#
err "to-stderr\n" eq check
st 3 eq check

; 3. both forms agree on stdout for a simple echo
"echo same-output" shell out# _# _#
["echo" "same-output"] shell out2# _# _#
out out2 eq check

; 4. large output (~1.3MB) must survive: the string, its length, and use —
; this is the exact shape that segfaulted before the GC-root fix
["seq" "1" "200000"] shell out# _# _#
out length 1288895 eq check

; 5. GC inside a for body over a collection: write 300k generated lines to a
; file, then fold the file while iterating the walk result — the collection
; lives across allocating bodies
["sh" "-c" "seq 1 300000"] shell out# _# _#
"/tmp/t38big.txt" out write_file
0 total!
countbig: p!
  p 0 stepl file_fold_lines total add total! ret
stepl: acc! _!
  acc 1 add ret
"/tmp" "t38big.txt" walk length 1 eq check
"/tmp" "t38big.txt" walk countbig for
total 300000 eq check

"shell-ok" print
ret
